## Feature #4 Increment 3 (spec §4.4) — CLOSE→Err, recv side: when the queue's
## recv side is closed (the consumer is gone), a `send` resolves
## `Err(QueueClosed)` instead of parking forever on a full queue. This is the
## send half of closing Go's forgotten-sender leak — a producer blocked on a
## queue whose consumer has abandoned it is told the channel is done.
##
## SCOPE (honest, same as pin 427): proves the close→Err RETURN SEMANTICS, NOT
## full leak-free ownership cleanup — the surviving `tx` ref is not released by
## the synthesized future drop (only `__sub_i` sub-futures are). Scoped to the
## close→Err codepath; full leak-free close is a field-drop follow-up.
##
## CLOSE TRIGGER (same honest v1 note as pin 427): `ruxen_queue_receiver_drop`
## is the C logic `AsyncReceiver`'s `def drop` runs (receiver_refs 1→0 →
## recv_closed → wake a parked send). We invoke it directly because a
## stdlib-class END local is not drop-elaborated at a deterministic mid-program
## point (the drop-rooting follow-up). Here we close the recv side BEFORE the
## send so `send_step` observes `recv_closed` and returns CLOSED → the send
## resolves `Err(QueueClosed)` — exercising the same close→Err path a parked
## Suspend-send would hit when the receiver drops while it waits.
##
## `closed_code` maps the send result to 1 on `Err(QueueClosed)`, proving the
## Err arm fired.

async def producer(tx: AsyncSender) -> Int
  let r = tx.send(99).await
  closed_code(r)
end

def closed_code(r: Result[Int, QueueError]) -> Int
  match r
    Ok(_)  -> 0
    Err(_) -> 1
  end
end

def main
  puts "queue start"
  let qp = ruxen_queue_new(1, Overflow.Suspend)
  let tx = AsyncSender.new(qp)
  # Close the recv side — the C logic AsyncReceiver's `def drop` runs.
  let _close = ruxen_queue_receiver_drop(qp)
  let code = block_on(producer(tx))
  puts "closed=#{code}"
  puts "queue done"
end
